# Kafka core concepts: exercises

Try these without looking at `answers.md`.

## Conceptual

1. In your own words, what is the "log" at the heart of Kafka, and what are two
   consequences of data being append-only?
   
   The log concept in Kafka concerns the core idea that consists of a sequence of records and does not overwrite themselves and just append at the end. 
   
2. What is the main difference between how a classic message queue and Kafka treat a
   message after it has been read?
   
   A classic message is deleted right after the its processing by a consumer. While Kafka messages are stored and retained for a configurable period of time. 
   
3. Match each term to its role: broker, cluster, topic, producer, consumer.

	broker - node that is the Kafka server storing data and serving requests
	cluster - group of nodes working together
	topic - specified type of message processing
	producer - who sends messages to a topic
	consumer - who gets messages from a topic and process it
	
4. What are bootstrap servers, and why do you not have to list every broker in the
   cluster when configuring a client?
   
   Newer Kafka cluster contains bootstrap servers nativelly and this is the way a consumer/service finds the Kafka cluster and its structure, such as its address, and its layout format, what are the brokers that do something and not other.
   
5. A message in Kafka has a key, a value, a timestamp, and headers. Which part is the
   actual payload, and what does Kafka itself understand about its contents?
   
   The actual payload of a Kafka message is the value. And Kafka understand it as pure bits. 
   
6. What job did ZooKeeper do in older Kafka, and what replaced it in modern Kafka?

	ZooKeeper used to manage the Kafka cluster, but it was replaced to a nativelly builtin Kafka Raft (KRaft).
	
7. Give two situations where Kafka is a good fit and one where a direct HTTP or gRPC
   call would be better.
   
   Declouped services and asynchronous communications are good fits for Kafka. While services that depends on request/response style is not a good fit for Kafka.
   
8. Three different services all need to react to the same `orders` events, each doing
   something different. Explain why Kafka handles this well.
   
   Kafka handles this well because different consumers can be related to the same Kafka broker and the queue will be consumed without duplicatas such as consuming the same event.

## Hands-on thinking

9. Draw (on paper or in text) a simple diagram: two producers writing to a topic
   `payments`, a cluster of three brokers, and two consumers reading the topic.
   Label where the messages are stored.
10. Retention is set to 7 days on a topic. A consumer reads a message today. Is the
    message gone after it is read? When would it actually disappear?
	
	The message is not deleted because Kafka will maintain that message during the retention period and other consumers can read that same messagem, if desired. Just a new log will be emmited when the message is consumed by the consumer.
